13. Adding Formula Rows and Columns
Definition: Formula rows and columns perform calculations within the form layout.

Oracle describes a formula column that calculates a percentage variance between January and February Sales.
Illustrative example
Account	January	February	Change %
Revenue	100,000	120,000	20%
Expenses	60,000	66,000	10%

For this illustration:
\[
\text{Change \%}=\frac{\text{February}-\text{January}}{\text{January}}\times100
\]

This arithmetic example illustrates the purpose of a formula column; it is not a literal FCCS formula syntax example.
Procedure from Oracle
1. Open the form and select Layout.
2. Right-click Rows or Columns.
3. Select Add Formula Row or Add Formula Column.
4. Enter the Formula Label.
5. Configure Segment Properties.
6. Select the Formula Data Type.
7. Open the formula editor.
8. Define the formula.
9. Click Validate.
10. Click OK and save the form.
Supported result types include Currency, Non-Currency, Percentage, Smart List, Date, and Text.

Reference: Chapter 19, page 33.
14. Defining Page Axis and POV
Oracle describes configuring both areas through the Layout tab.
Procedure
1. Open the form and select Layout.
2. Drag the required dimension to Page.
3. Select its members.
4. Repeat for additional Page dimensions.
5. Configure the Page dimension properties.
6. Move the required dimensions to POV.
7. Select POV members.
8. Save or Finish.
Key distinction: POV identifies a particular data intersection, while the Page Axis lets users switch among configured member selections.

Reference: Chapter 19, page 34.
15. SingleTime Dimension
This is an important feature in Dense/Sparse Optimized applications.

Definition: SingleTime is a replacement dimension that combines Year and Period selections for use in forms.

For example, the Year and Period members can be represented together as Jul2022.

Oracle specifies that SingleTime is available in forms and forms accessed through Smart View. It does not replace how Year and Period data is stored.
Two important functions
Function	Purpose
Relative	Displays a period at an offset from a selected period
RelativeRange	Displays a range of periods relative to a selected period

Oracle provides these examples:
- Relative(Mar 2022, 5) displays August 2022.
- RelativeRange(Jan 2020, 2) displays January, February, and March 2020.
Configuration
1. Enable Combine Year and Period as a Single Dimension during application creation or through Enable Features.
2. Create a data form.
3. Select SingleTime under Replacement Dimensions.
4. Configure Year and Period on the same row or column axis.
5. Use the supported Relative or RelativeRange functions.
6. Save and test the form.
Oracle specifies that Year and Period must be on the same axis, without individual selections, for SingleTime functionality.

Reference: Chapter 19, pages 34–36.
